 %Assuming a weak coupling between the blade structure and the aerodynamic excitation, and under the hypothesis of linear behavior of the blade structure with harmonic motion, the assessment of these phenomena relies on the so-called Generalized Aerodynamic Forces (GAFs), which are obtained by projecting the unsteady pressure distribution onto the structural vibration modes of the blade. The GAFs provide the link between the aerodynamic forcing and the structural response and are therefore key quantities in the evaluation of blade vibration amplitudes, high-cycle fatigue risk and mechanical integrity. Because these quantities depend on both the spatial distribution and temporal evolution of the pressure field, their accurate prediction requires a reliable representation of the underlying flow dynamics.
 
